  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none",
    "relation": "discusses",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "Tests class-wise unlearning under data bias in image classifiers, a new setting for the claim. It measures forgetting and retained accuracy, but does not test whether recovery attacks work.",
    "gate_note": "The study reports unlearning outcomes for NegGrad and other methods, including CUPID, on biased image-classification datasets. It does not apply fine-tuning recovery attacks, activation ablation, quantization, or prompting attacks. The shortcut-accuracy and linear-probe results are recorded as findings outside the specified versions because they concern bias features, not recovery of the forgotten class knowledge.",
    "test_reason": "The claim concerns LLM unlearning of hazardous or copyrighted knowledge and whether recovery attacks restore it. This study evaluates class-wise unlearning in ResNet-50 image classifiers trained on biased datasets (Waterbirds, BAR, Biased NICO++). It applies no recovery attack: no relearning fine-tuning, activation ablation, quantization or prompting. Its Table 1/2 rows mainly compare the authors' own method (CUPID) with baselines, which the rules exclude as rows. That comparison is in a different kind of system and measures no attack resistance, so it does not test version A or F. Its finding that NegGrad erases the shortcut rather than the class is an analogue of shallow unlearning in a different construct, recorded under fits_no_version.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Mostly correct. The authors conclude that standard unlearning on biased models shows 'shortcut unlearning': bias-aligned samples are hard to forget, and the model erases the shortcut attribute rather than the class. Unlearning can even improve accuracy on bias-conflicting samples of the forgotten class. They propose CUPID (sharpness-based partitioning, causal pathway identification, targeted gradient routing). They report it achieves the lowest forget accuracy and the most balanced forgetting on Waterbirds, BAR and Biased NICO++, without needing the retain set.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study evaluates class-wise unlearning in ResNet-50 image classifiers under synthetic or dataset-specific bias, and proposes CUPID's sharpness partitioning and pathway-targeted updates. It does not evaluate LLM unlearning or the recovery attacks in the claim.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The study reports performance differences across datasets and between heavily biased training-set evaluation and unbiased test-set evaluation. CUPID uses k = 5%, perturbation size η = 10−3, and masking ratio τp = 50%.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Under NegGrad on biased ResNet-50 classifiers, bias-conflicting forget samples are forgotten easily while bias-aligned ones resist forgetting. Accuracy on the bias-conflicting forget subset rises after unlearning. Linear-probe accuracy for the bias label drops significantly, so the unlearning mainly removed the shortcut rather than the class.",
        "proposed_claim": "In biased classifiers, gradient-ascent unlearning erases the spurious shortcut rather than the target class's causal features ('shortcut unlearning'). Apparent forgetting is therefore shallow with respect to the class concept.",
        "row_ids": [
          "R25",
          "R26"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "controls": "y",
    "capability_check": "y",
    "uncertainty_reported": "n",
    "code_released": "not stated",
    "peer_reviewed": "AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence",
    "problems": "The experiments use image classifiers rather than LLMs and do not test recovery attacks. The text says one class is randomly selected from each dataset; no uncertainty estimates are reported. The shortcut analysis includes figure-based results without numerical values in the text.",
    "rows_left_out": "Other unlearning methods and additional metrics in Tables 1 and 2 were not extracted; the selected table rows cover CUPID and NegGrad across both evaluation sets and all three datasets. Ablations and CUPID-only component comparisons were omitted because they do not directly test the recovery attacks or the measured unlearning-and-retained-accuracy component of version A.",
